[SPEAKER_02]: But now that's done.

387
00:24:13,985 --> 00:24:15,086
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it's irreversible.

388
00:24:20,208 --> 00:24:32,458
[SPEAKER_00]: But as... as someone starting out, when you read all saṅkharas of Nietzsche, means anything... self... that's what I'm saying, all self-determinations.

389
00:24:32,518 --> 00:24:34,580
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything that determines your sense of self.

390
00:24:34,660 --> 00:24:35,801
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, yes, yes.

391
00:24:36,021 --> 00:24:41,365
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the plurality of saṅkharas, it's through the singularity of the sense of self.

392
00:24:41,765 --> 00:24:43,807
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you can only see it rightly.

393
00:24:43,847 --> 00:24:46,669
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like, that's impermanent, that's impermanent, that's impermanent, that's impermanent.

394
00:24:46,969 --> 00:24:47,250
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus,

395
00:24:47,926 --> 00:24:48,767
[SPEAKER_02]: I am not my own.

396
00:24:48,867 --> 00:24:49,167
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

397
00:24:49,668 --> 00:24:50,569
[SPEAKER_02]: I am myself.

398
00:24:51,650 --> 00:24:57,816
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, I read about that I can't be my own, because I'm not my own master, as in, I am not in control of my own life, my senses, etc.

399
00:24:58,256 --> 00:25:05,424
[SPEAKER_02]: So, within that sense of I am, you then find all these other things that could be determining you, that are determining you, and then you realize they are unownable.

400
00:25:05,744 --> 00:25:07,986
[SPEAKER_02]: And the reason they are unownable is because they're plural.

401
00:25:09,925 --> 00:25:13,268
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, the singular mastery, the sense of self-mastery, is always singular.

402
00:25:13,288 --> 00:25:14,869
[SPEAKER_00]: As I was saying, self-determination.

403
00:25:14,949 --> 00:25:17,311
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

404
00:25:17,432 --> 00:25:18,392
[SPEAKER_00]: It undermines that.

405
00:25:18,412 --> 00:25:19,053
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

406
00:25:19,093 --> 00:25:20,614
[SPEAKER_02]: Self-determination.

407
00:25:20,654 --> 00:25:31,344
[SPEAKER_02]: No, that's an important point, because people would, even this, where we're speaking, would probably, could understand as, okay, so you just find these random things, and then, sort of, reflect how they cannot be yours.

408
00:25:32,793 --> 00:25:39,318
[SPEAKER_02]: That's accurate in theory, but in practice, you want to do that through your sense of self, not left, right, up or down, before or after.

409
00:25:39,338 --> 00:25:40,739
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, what is determining this thing?

410
00:25:40,779 --> 00:25:41,159
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

411
00:25:41,199 --> 00:25:44,582
[SPEAKER_02]: So, first, you have to kind of start in the reverse order, the right order.

412
00:25:44,882 --> 00:25:49,986
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I am, I am such-and-such, I am such-and-such in such-and-such situation.

413
00:25:50,967 --> 00:25:54,770
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, there is obviously some emotional engagement, because there has to be something felt.

414
00:25:56,418 --> 00:26:01,003
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the basis through which you start looking at things that are dear to you.

415
00:26:01,263 --> 00:26:13,537
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why, like, mindfulness of death, done rightly, will uproot anything, everything, because it pertains to the absolute fundamental appropriation of your experience as a whole, of the body, senses, all of that.

416
00:26:20,932 --> 00:26:26,435
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's always a kind of... Those things will be seen as determinations...

417
00:26:26,475 --> 00:26:26,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

418
00:26:26,695 --> 00:26:28,095
[SPEAKER_01]: ...only through the sense of self.

419
00:26:28,115 --> 00:26:29,316
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what determination is.

420
00:26:29,396 --> 00:26:29,616
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

421
00:26:29,676 --> 00:26:31,677
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like negative.

422
00:26:31,697 --> 00:26:32,137
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

423
00:26:32,177 --> 00:26:34,018
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why people get confused.

424
00:26:34,038 --> 00:26:43,563
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why the Buddha himself said, when he described various aggregates, he said, this is what matter is, this is what feeling is, this is what perception is, but determination is in consciousness.

425
00:26:43,603 --> 00:26:44,763
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, what is matter?

426
00:26:44,783 --> 00:26:45,524
[SPEAKER_02]: This is matter.

427
00:26:45,844 --> 00:26:46,844
[SPEAKER_02]: What is determination?

428
00:26:46,904 --> 00:26:48,125
[SPEAKER_02]: And then list the five aggregates.

429
00:26:48,145 --> 00:26:48,385
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

430
00:26:49,185 --> 00:26:53,987
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yes, everything can be a determination if your sense of self depends on it.

431
00:26:54,027 --> 00:26:58,569
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means, in order to see it as a determination, you need to see it through your sense of self.

432
00:26:58,649 --> 00:27:02,050
[SPEAKER_02]: Not like, oh, yes, look, out there, that's impermanent.

433
00:27:02,090 --> 00:27:06,092
[SPEAKER_00]: It's seeing the context where you're at.

434
00:27:06,332 --> 00:27:07,552
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the context is self.

435
00:27:07,772 --> 00:27:07,972
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

436
00:27:08,893 --> 00:27:09,253
[SPEAKER_00]: It is.

437
00:27:09,353 --> 00:27:11,434
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the context where I start from.

438
00:27:11,534 --> 00:27:11,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

439
00:27:11,774 --> 00:27:12,514
[SPEAKER_00]: It's myself.

440
00:27:12,534 --> 00:27:14,495
[SPEAKER_02]: So, even if you look at the same thing,

441
00:27:15,762 --> 00:27:21,990
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you don't look at it through the sense of self, through the right context, you're not seeing it as a determination.

442
00:27:22,010 --> 00:27:24,914
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it isn't determining a sense of self.

443
00:27:24,994 --> 00:27:30,402
[SPEAKER_02]: Because determination is more of a relationship between your sense of self and things that make your sense of self.

444
00:27:35,725 --> 00:27:40,689
[SPEAKER_01]: But that pertains to... to the sense of self, because that's the sole problem.

445
00:27:40,809 --> 00:27:50,356
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing that context of self is determined by the content that I'm taking as pertaining to me, mine.

446
00:27:51,857 --> 00:27:55,379
[SPEAKER_00]: That seeing that undermines the ignorance of that situation.

447
00:27:55,579 --> 00:27:55,800
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

448
00:27:56,040 --> 00:27:57,501
[SPEAKER_02]: It's seen in that order.

449
00:27:59,022 --> 00:28:01,824
[SPEAKER_02]: Because sense of self depends upon certain

450
00:28:06,508 --> 00:28:11,494
[SPEAKER_02]: So, now you go and look at the individual, the crown, the cape, the throne, all these things.

451
00:28:11,534 --> 00:28:14,057
[SPEAKER_02]: But now, they are the things that are determination for him.

452
00:28:23,940 --> 00:28:26,821
[SPEAKER_02]: And even if you are king yourself, they are determining you.

453
00:28:27,281 --> 00:28:32,444
[SPEAKER_02]: But the only way to see them as determination is first to see them through your kinghood, so to speak.

454
00:28:32,904 --> 00:28:43,048
[SPEAKER_02]: Through your sense of self, what you are, and then see how this sense of self that's present and enduring, simultaneously, is now, in the right order, determined by these things at the same time.

455
00:28:43,649 --> 00:28:45,730
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not determined by you choosing to be king.

456
00:28:46,170 --> 00:28:50,432
[SPEAKER_02]: It's determined by you choosing to own these things that, oh, can be taken away.

457
00:28:50,752 --> 00:28:53,073
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, your sense of, I'm a king, can also

458
00:28:59,843 --> 00:29:01,604
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you forget that, it would be like, okay, I'm a king.

459
00:29:02,345 --> 00:29:04,167
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, and now you start examining your crown.

460
00:29:04,187 --> 00:29:05,748
[SPEAKER_02]: You do... oh, this crown is impermanent.

461
00:29:05,768 --> 00:29:06,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Focus on it.

462
00:29:06,468 --> 00:29:07,309
[SPEAKER_01]: This is impermanent.

463
00:29:07,369 --> 00:29:08,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Focus, focus, focus, focus.

464
00:29:08,870 --> 00:29:11,953
[SPEAKER_02]: Look at what it's made of to prove that the whole is impermanent.

465
00:29:12,333 --> 00:29:19,119
[SPEAKER_02]: And then all these things... And never ever, for a single second, do things in the right order whereby first you start, I am a king.

466
00:29:19,979 --> 00:29:21,180
[SPEAKER_02]: That's determined by this.

467
00:29:21,681 --> 00:29:23,983
[SPEAKER_02]: Not, I am a king that has all of this.

468
00:29:24,423 --> 00:29:26,505
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's like, I am, this is mine.

469
00:29:26,685 --> 00:29:27,385
[SPEAKER_02]: It's other way around.

470
00:29:28,503 --> 00:29:31,116
[SPEAKER_02]: Because there are things that are mine, through that sense of I am.

471
00:29:33,110 --> 00:29:38,253
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you don't start with, I'm a king, and now I get my... And now I go to the next subject.

472
00:29:38,313 --> 00:29:39,054
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm a king.

473
00:29:39,094 --> 00:29:40,275
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why I keep insisting...

474
00:29:40,315 --> 00:29:41,816
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm first, and everything else second.

475
00:29:41,856 --> 00:29:45,518
[SPEAKER_01]: Although, actually, probably, that's probably what the patujana would assume.

476
00:29:45,538 --> 00:29:46,279
[SPEAKER_01]: They would think that.

477
00:29:46,299 --> 00:29:50,461
[SPEAKER_01]: They would think, first of all, this is who I am, and then, that's why I'm given these things.

478
00:29:50,822 --> 00:29:53,704
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why they will still be a patujana, for as long as they don't approve that view.

479
00:29:53,844 --> 00:29:57,686
[SPEAKER_02]: Realizing that... And that's why whenever we do a contemplation, whenever we do

480
00:29:59,047 --> 00:30:08,480
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, like, when we record these things, it's always like, I keep insisting on being aware of your situation, and then, while you're aware of situation, you look at other things within that.

481
00:30:09,001 --> 00:30:10,804
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's doing exactly the same principle.

482
00:30:10,824 --> 00:30:13,007
[SPEAKER_02]: So, while I am a king,

483
00:30:14,407 --> 00:30:20,392
[SPEAKER_02]: There are all these things that I own, because of which I'm a king, while I am a king right here, right now.

484
00:30:20,773 --> 00:30:24,636
[SPEAKER_02]: So, while I'm king right here, right now, what if I were to lose my castle?

485
00:30:25,177 --> 00:30:27,659
[SPEAKER_02]: I would not be a king anymore, while I'm a king right now.

486
00:30:28,119 --> 00:30:34,385
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you will also be unable to remain indifferent to your ownership or loss of ownership.

487
00:30:36,447 --> 00:30:40,010
[SPEAKER_02]: The only true way to be indifferent towards it is to abandon any sense of ownership.

488
00:30:44,857 --> 00:31:02,622
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you start looking for ownership and abandoning ownership, like undoing the saṅkharas and whatnot, not through the sense of self, but as an afterthought, as a management technique, as a meditation technique, as a magical hope that it will happen at the end of it, your sense of self remains unaffected, which means you'll be owning that now.

489
00:31:03,743 --> 00:31:05,563
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's exactly why people would get upset.

490
00:31:05,603 --> 00:31:06,303
[SPEAKER_02]: And you basically...

491
00:31:06,323 --> 00:31:11,325
[SPEAKER_02]: If somebody, yeah, disagrees, even on the level, like, says your meditation technique is wrong, you get upset.

492
00:31:13,148 --> 00:31:20,191
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if I'm wrong in saying that you are wrong, if you are getting upset, that's a problem... that should be a problem for you.

493
00:31:20,211 --> 00:31:21,111
[SPEAKER_02]: That's a red flag.

494
00:31:21,411 --> 00:31:27,353
[SPEAKER_02]: If you come across someone else's views and you get upset on account of that, that should tell you a lot.

495
00:31:28,814 --> 00:31:30,095
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's a work for you to do.

496
00:31:31,835 --> 00:31:36,977
[SPEAKER_02]: Instead, if you truly understood, you will be able to see wrong views as wrong views and right views as right views.

497
00:31:37,417 --> 00:31:41,319
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if somebody comes and says you are wrong, you will be able to see whether they are wrong in saying that,

498
00:31:42,142 --> 00:31:46,666
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, whether they are right in saying that, in which case, you'll be more than happy to upgrade your behavior.

499
00:31:49,289 --> 00:31:54,834
[SPEAKER_02]: But it needs to be taken on that personal level, which is initially unpleasant, and that's why everybody refuses it.

500
00:31:56,335 --> 00:32:05,283
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why we just discussed that before, like, how the most rational people would abandon the whole intellect, if something pokes, triggers their feelings.

501
00:32:13,489 --> 00:32:16,932
[SPEAKER_02]: It's unpleasant for me, as a king, to think how I'm a king.

502
00:32:16,972 --> 00:32:21,296
[SPEAKER_02]: Not because I'm a king, but because I have all these things that define me as a king.

503
00:32:22,377 --> 00:32:30,845
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, I can think about those things as an afterthought, when I don't really think through the feeling of being threatened as a king.

504
00:32:30,905 --> 00:32:34,088
[SPEAKER_02]: No, I just think and maybe ponder on the days when I have energy.

505
00:32:34,888 --> 00:32:36,670
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's usually how most people practice the dhamma.

506
00:32:39,441 --> 00:32:43,603
[SPEAKER_02]: When I have something particular to deal with, or, like, you know, when I feel like it.

507
00:32:44,663 --> 00:32:45,304
[SPEAKER_02]: But, no.

508
00:32:45,404 --> 00:32:50,026
[SPEAKER_02]: Needs to be done on that... That's why, like, existentially, I'm prey to suffering, as they would have described.

509
00:32:50,066 --> 00:32:58,830
[SPEAKER_02]: Means, you're always on the verge of anxiety, recognizing that, yeah, all this can be uprooted in a finger snap, and you cannot even conceive how to prevent it.

510
00:33:00,130 --> 00:33:10,938
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if that's your context, you would be seeing everything else that's kind of going against it, which is any form of ownership, any form of lust, passion, anger, distraction, carelessness.

511
00:33:11,718 --> 00:33:12,979
[SPEAKER_02]: It's against that context.

512
00:33:13,300 --> 00:33:14,561
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it's unwholesome.

513
00:33:18,503 --> 00:33:19,844
[SPEAKER_00]: It's against that context?

514
00:33:19,965 --> 00:33:20,525
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

515
00:33:20,585 --> 00:33:23,107
[SPEAKER_00]: Of things being... yourself being determined?

516
00:33:23,127 --> 00:33:23,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

517
00:33:30,252 --> 00:33:30,813
[SPEAKER_02]: You know?

518
00:33:30,833 --> 00:33:33,135
[SPEAKER_02]: You are because you keep taking things to be yours.

519
00:33:34,116 --> 00:33:36,198
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's where that right order of things depends.

520
00:33:36,218 --> 00:33:37,619
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you'll be looking at the same thing.

521
00:33:37,659 --> 00:33:40,241
[SPEAKER_02]: It will be a completely wrong thing if you don't see it through the right order.

522
00:33:41,162 --> 00:33:44,065
[SPEAKER_02]: That's when people in the suttas would understand the Buddha first time.

523
00:33:44,746 --> 00:33:50,251
[SPEAKER_02]: You'll be like, wow, it's like putting that which was second, putting it first, and putting first which was second.

524
00:33:50,411 --> 00:33:50,971
[SPEAKER_02]: It's all there.

525
00:33:51,011 --> 00:33:52,653
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, there's nothing new can be introduced.

526
00:33:52,673 --> 00:33:54,415
[SPEAKER_02]: But, oh, but I thought,

527
00:33:57,164 --> 00:33:58,304
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it goes on to say that all

528
00:34:26,998 --> 00:34:28,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Sankhāras are dukkha.

529
00:34:29,520 --> 00:34:31,862
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

530
00:34:31,982 --> 00:34:33,843
[SPEAKER_02]: All sankhāras are unownable.

531
00:34:33,863 --> 00:34:34,043
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

532
00:34:34,304 --> 00:34:37,026
[SPEAKER_02]: All sankhāras are dukkha, because they're unownable.

533
00:34:37,646 --> 00:34:45,472
[SPEAKER_02]: So, because all things, through your sense of self, they determine your sense of self, cannot... you cannot be their master.

534
00:34:46,433 --> 00:34:48,034
[SPEAKER_02]: They are unownable because of that.

535
00:34:48,094 --> 00:34:48,815
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't own them.

536
00:34:49,255 --> 00:34:51,857
[SPEAKER_02]: You took the ownership given to you for granted.

537
00:34:53,313 --> 00:34:59,758
[SPEAKER_02]: But the fact that ownership was given to you means you can't own it, because it can be taken away, like a borrowed gold, borrowed jewelry, they would say.

538
00:35:00,138 --> 00:35:09,626
[SPEAKER_02]: So, because all these saṅkharas, through my sense of self, cannot be owned, that's why they cannot be like a basis for lasting satisfaction, for pleasure.

539
00:35:09,666 --> 00:35:10,767
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why they are suffering.

540
00:35:10,787 --> 00:35:11,848
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why they are unpleasant.

541
00:35:12,308 --> 00:35:15,291
[SPEAKER_02]: Because of that, this sense of self is thus up.

542
00:35:16,131 --> 00:35:19,494
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like the conclusion.

543
00:35:20,975 --> 00:35:24,697
[SPEAKER_00]: So, seeing that this sense of self... Yeah, it's a direct recognition.

544
00:35:24,717 --> 00:35:30,541
[SPEAKER_00]: ...determined by these things, thus... That sense of self is then automatically not felt as mine.

545
00:35:30,621 --> 00:35:32,983
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because it's the thing which is not mine.

546
00:35:33,203 --> 00:35:33,423
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

547
00:35:33,883 --> 00:35:34,464
[SPEAKER_00]: All dhammas.

548
00:35:35,044 --> 00:35:40,688
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why I keep saying, like, if you see... if you see anicca and dukkha correctly, you don't then have to go and figure out anatta.

549
00:35:41,369 --> 00:35:51,636
[SPEAKER_02]: Seeing anicca and dukkha correctly means seeing anicca and dukkha through your sense of self, which then means seeing anicca and dukkha, this sense of self, through which I'm seeing this, cannot be mine.

550
00:35:51,656 --> 00:35:54,078
[SPEAKER_02]: So your experience as a whole is the experience of anatta.

551
00:35:54,318 --> 00:36:07,223
[SPEAKER_01]: But even seeing impermanence and suffering as being bound up with each other and inseparable, I think a lot of people think... See it out there.

552
00:36:07,403 --> 00:36:11,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, they see that things are kind of... That's why it's not...

553
00:36:11,865 --> 00:36:13,185
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not the Amitabha who talks about it.

554
00:36:13,205 --> 00:36:14,486
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why I keep saying it's unownable.

555
00:36:15,186 --> 00:36:20,808
[SPEAKER_02]: In fact, the feeling that the knee is actually just in this state of flux is actually quite pleasant.

556
00:36:21,228 --> 00:36:22,009
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure.

557
00:36:22,389 --> 00:36:26,032
[SPEAKER_02]: Or when people say, look, the modern science has proven everything is impermanent.

558
00:36:26,332 --> 00:36:26,592
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

559
00:36:26,973 --> 00:36:28,414
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not what impermanence is.

560
00:36:29,335 --> 00:36:31,416
[SPEAKER_02]: Impermanence is un-ownability.

561
00:36:31,877 --> 00:36:33,158
[SPEAKER_02]: Modern science cannot prove that.

562
00:36:34,319 --> 00:36:38,162
[SPEAKER_02]: Which can only be felt as... On the level of ownership or non-ownership, yeah.

563
00:36:39,063 --> 00:36:42,884
[SPEAKER_02]: You can say, look, this is impermanent, this is impermanent, this is impermanent, this is impermanent.

564
00:36:42,924 --> 00:36:49,465
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything is impermanent, but it's still, it's not seen as impermanent things that determine me right here, right now.

565
00:36:49,485 --> 00:36:52,346
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just seen in relation to me right here, right now.

566
00:36:52,386 --> 00:36:58,167
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, I'm saying, I am, and all these things that can be mine are not mine, but I still am, basically.

567
00:36:58,647 --> 00:37:01,128
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in order to see anicca and dukkha correctly,

